1
00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.837
Welcome to DSPy: Build and

2
00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:07.674
Optimize Agentic Apps
built in partnership with Databricks.

3
00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:11.277
In this course
you'll learn how to build and optimize

4
00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:14.280
your genAI application using DSPy.

5
00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:16.883
When you start building complex
AI apps, one

6
00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:19.919
big challenge is writing
good prompts for an LLM.

7
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.857
I'll often try dozens of prompts tweaking
words, changing formats,

8
00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:25.692
hoping to get better results.

9
00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:28.862
But this process takes a lot of time,
and the prompts often

10
00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:31.865
even break
when you change the underlying LLM.

11
00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:35.335
DSPy streamlines and optimizes
this whole process.

12
00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:39.005
You define what inputs your model needs
and what outputs you return,

13
00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:42.675
and also provide a data set of inputs
and desired outputs.

14
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.046
DSPy can then optimize your AI programs

15
00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:49.049
to get better performance
with much less manual work.

16
00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:54.816
I'm delighted to introduce the instructor Chen Qian
who is a software engineer at Databricks,

17
00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:57.957
and co-leads the developments of DSPy.

18
00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.860
Thanks Andrew. In this course you will learn

19
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.860
how to build AI programs with DSPy.
DSPy has two main building blocks:

20
00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:10.570
signature and module.
When you are trying to build a component

21
00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:14.841
of an application
specifying the signature tells a system

22
00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:18.311
what inputs and outputs
to expect from the

23
00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:21.514
LLM component. For example, a sentiment

24
00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:26.748
analysis program will have a syntax as an
input and an integer representing a sentiment

25
00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:28.755
as the output.

26
00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:32.625
A module then uses those signatures
to actually call a language

27
00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:34.127
model and get results.

28
00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:39.099
Sometimes, an app doesn't work but we are unsure why.

29
00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:43.169
In this course you will also learn to use MLflow tracing

30
00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:48.007
to help you see exactly what is going on, each step of the application.

31
00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:50.743
What data was used, what tools were called

32
00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:54.414
what the model returned and where things broke.

33
00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:59.252
With just one line of code, you can turn on this tracing feature for your app.

34
00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:02.388
One neat use DSPy is in optimizing agentic

35
00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:03.590
workflows.

36
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.226
If you have a complex workflow
that takes

37
00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:09.529
an input and uses
multiple steps of processing

38
00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:13.399
to generate the outputs, maybe using an LLM
on multiple of these steps,

39
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:18.538
then DSPy's optimizer can take your agents
as well as an evaluation

40
00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:22.575
datasets and metric, and based on that,
automatically search for better

41
00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:27.147
prompts for all those steps. I've seen it
sometimes build really high-quality few-shot

42
00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:31.451
prompts from your data
in a way that is far better

43
00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:35.855
than I, as a human could likely have
achieved by hand. In this course,

44
00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:39.792
you use these DSPy's optimizer
to optimize prompts in a RAG app

45
00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:42.328
that answers Wikipedia questions.

46
00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.764
Many people have worked
to create this course.

47
00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:49.764
I'd like to thank Omar Khattab
Cathy Yin, Krista Opsahl-Ong

48
00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:55.108
and Tomu Hirata for Databricks.
From DeepLearning.AI,

49
00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:59.812
Esmaeil Gargari and Brendan Brown
also contributed to this course.

50
00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:03.316
The first lesson will be an introduction
to DSPy.

51
00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:07.787
One surprising thing about DSPy is
how few lines of code it takes implement,

52
00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:12.525
and that it essentially automates
the prompt engineering process.

53
00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.461
Please go on to the next
video to see how this works.

